 runs a coordinated, non-synchronous failover: the primary pauses writes, waits for a replica to consume the replication stream, demotes itself, then sends PSYNC FAILOVER; it rolls back if rejected."},{"claim_id":"cluster-manual","evidence_ids":["ev_293963f78c22e7e3d6b6d18b1f66fe682a850f474ea7408bf6612e8655ace4d6"],"text":"In Valkey Cluster, CLUSTER FAILOVER performed on a replica avoids data loss by switching clients only after the new primary has processed the old primary's replication stream; writes block during failover."},{"claim_id":"sentinel-consistency","evidence_ids":["ev_225b0ec416616ff80113db29c733d63d7b078fa691e1af482a9aaae7db848ce9","ev_1ab18e398dfa6e4222c086c9aa5bdf186ece9d9768173944e21c8696e3c5168c"],"text":"With Sentinel, Valkey is eventually consistent with last-failover-wins merging, so acknowledged writes can be lost; Sentinel guarantees liveness and per-failover distinct configuration epochs."}],"executed_lambda_version":"8","expected_behavior":"answer","generation_id":"sha256:6760b22e3e09ac0e2ac64c9c23b3a77733f5bd1f8f8c9704c66d2c69d1362b60","index":9,"latency_ms":20002.480592,"message":null,"outcome":"answer","preflight_path":"cases/09-real-replication-failover/preflight.json","preflight_sha256":"sha256:a2ed4f14785adb21318b6501aff50e5a2b33c7b23a81e18ea133098dfc85536c","protocol_verdict":"pass","question":"How do Valkey replication and failover behave?","request_fence":1,"request_id":"req_deployed-eval-990c3214c4e9207f5e67c964dd9fdcbfe8e9067d2ed5d3d2268a2ca72a89ba02","request_revision":2,"request_sha256":"sha256:735de1f65abfcf1860aad0651512605d14f0835692211591447e4f6afd20ce98","response_sha256":"sha256:fd733151d0012f8be60c55a980524e0dadbfbd5bc2baddfa73e17856ca476acc","result_path":"cases/09-real-replication-failover/results/09-real-replication-failover.json","result_sha256":"sha256:8682fca834c87f6383c4b0765d69e28dc353ea80e5440b8547e97c158285466c","route":"static","semantic_verdict":"not_evaluated","terminal_path":null,"terminal_sha256":null},{"at